<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ancient Greek philosophy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Greek philosophy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ancient Greek philosophy arose in the 6th century BCE and dealt with astronomy, mathematics, ethics, metaphysics, logic, biology, rhetoric and politics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> regarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales of Miletus</a> as the first philosopher, and the thinkers before the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> are conventionally called the pre-Socratics. Socrates, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and Aristotle marked a new approach, and after the wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> came Hellenistic schools such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stoicism">Stoicism</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeno-of-citium">Zeno of Citium</a>, and Epicureanism. Greek philosophy evolved into Roman philosophy, and lines of influence run from it to early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-philosophy">Islamic philosophy</a>, medieval scholasticism, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ancient-greek-philosophy.jpg" alt="Ancient Greek philosophy" width="330" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raphael · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:&quot;The_School_of_Athens&quot;_by_Raffaello_Sanzio_da_Urbino.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
